    "section_evoo_v3_faq_rRJhLR": {
      "type": "section-evoo-v3-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "faq_item_BFc6Am": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is Berberine and what is A-Berine specifically?",
            "answer": "<p>Berberine is a bioactive alkaloid found in the roots, bark, and stems of plants from the *Berberis* family. It has been used in traditional Ayurvedic and Chinese medicine for centuries. AIWO A-Berine contains Berberis Extract standardised to 10% Berberine - 500 mg per film-coated tablet - manufactured at an ISO-certified, FSSAI-licensed facility.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_pgG9dR": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does AMPK activation mean?",
            "answer": "<p>AMPK (AMP-Activated Protein Kinase) is an enzyme found in the brain, muscle, kidney, heart, and liver that plays a central role in regulating how your body produces and uses energy. Berberine activates AMPK, which supports glucose uptake, reduces sugar production in the liver, and influences lipid metabolism. Research refers to AMPK as the body's \"metabolic master switch.\"<br/></p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_zet6LP": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who is this most useful for?",
            "answer": "<p>A-Berine is formulated for adults looking to support metabolic health - particularly individuals who want to support glucose and lipid metabolism as part of a broader wellness protocol. Berberine research has primarily been conducted in individuals with elevated fasting blood sugar or those at metabolic risk. It is not appropriate as a general daily supplement for everyone.<br/></p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_Q6TUxh": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I take this with my current medication?",
            "answer": "<p>Not without checking first. Berberine interacts with several enzyme systems and may affect the metabolism of certain drugs including blood glucose-lowering medications, antihypertensives, and some oral contraceptives. People taking cyclosporine should not take Berberine. Always consult your physician before starting.<br/></p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_WyBt4K": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long before I notice a difference?",
            "answer": "<p>Clinical studies on Berberine have used supplementation periods of 8–12 weeks for metabolic outcomes. Allow at least 60–90 days of consistent daily use before assessing results. Results will vary based on starting health status, diet, and dosage.<br/></p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
